     line 1: unbounded universal (no one) in: Lesson (2026-10-10, overreach-lint soft spots): when a check names its own soft spots, those spots are the next corpus to build, because the named gap is usually a real one. Both soft spots the reach lint named after the 2026-10-10 deepening broke through: a bare number counted as a bound whatever it numbered (PR 156, step 3, a date), "no one" slipped past because "one" matched the count word list, and the punctuation-only clause split read a bounded clause plus its unbounded continuation as one bounded clause. Fixing them taught a second thing: tightening the clause split created a false positive at the seam, where a coordinated clause with only a bare pronoun ("none failed") read as unbounded over a domain its sibling had already bounded. So a split finer than before needs its own coreference rule at the seam, and the new rule needs its own re-break corpus the way the widening did.
     bound it to what was checked, or declare the line a norm (rule: / habit: / norm:).
